<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Coalition Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kokoomus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Coalition Party is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s main liberal-conservative party, founded in December 1918 by monarchist members of the old Finnish Party and Young Finnish Party. Long kept out of government during the Kekkonen era, it has since led several governments, and its members have included presidents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sauli-niinisto">Sauli Niinistö</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-stubb">Alexander Stubb</a>. It supports a market economy and Finland's membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded on 9 December 1918, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a>, by the majority of the Finnish Party and a minority of the Young Finnish Party. Both groups supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-frederick-charles-of-hesse">Prince Frederick Charles of Hesse</a> as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in a new monarchy. The republicans of the two parties had founded the National Progressive Party the previous day. The founding meeting, with over 600 representatives, called for a national coalition across old party lines and for lawful order without compromise with revolutionary aspirations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The party advocated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and, failing that, strong government powers within a republic. The monarchist aim failed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> became a parliamentary republic, in which the party argued for strong presidential powers. In the late 1920s and early 1930s fear of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> moved it towards a very conservative position. In the 1933 election it formed a coalition with the Patriotic People's Movement and lost 24 of its 42 seats. Under its new chair, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi">Juho Kusti Paasikivi</a>, it broke with that movement in 1934.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continuation-war">Continuation War</a> the party joined the wartime national unity governments. Paasikivi was prime minister from 1944 to 1946 and president from 1946 to 1956, and is remembered as the formulator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s foreign policy after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In 1951 the party changed its name from Kansallinen Kokoomuspuolue to Kansallinen Kokoomus, and in the 1950s it placed more emphasis on individual liberty and free-market reform. A minor split in 1958 produced the Christian Democrats.</p><p>From 1966 to 1987 the party was in opposition. By criticising the Finnish communists and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urho-kekkonen">Urho Kekkonen</a> of the Centre Party it lost the president's trust, and governments of the Centre Party and left-wing parties followed one another. In 1987 it had its best election result so far, and Harri Holkeri became its first prime minister since Paasikivi. It remained in government as a minor partner until 2003. Under Jyrki Katainen, chair from 2004 to 2014, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> became its main attribute, and in 2011 it was the largest party in parliament for the first time, with 44 of 200 seats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party is built from municipal and local chapters grouped into districts, together with women's, student and youth wings. A party conference, which meets every two years, elects the chair and a party council of 61 members. Its voters have come predominantly from urban areas, while rural areas tend to favour the Centre Party. Membership stood at 81,000 in 1970 and at 34,000 in 2016. The party is a member of the European People's Party.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Coalition Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Proportional representation produced coalition governments, often led by the Social Democrats, the Centre Party or the <b>National Coalition Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Parliament in 2003 proposed making either &quot;Maamme&quot; or the Finlandia Hymn the official anthem, a citizens' initiative of 2014 asked for the Finlandia Hymn, and in 2016 members of the <b>National Coalition Party</b> campaigned for it, but opinion in the party was divided and the campaign fell short.</blockquote><a href="/songs/maamme">Maamme</a></li></ul></section>
